Security-v2 migration notes
===========================

The security branch is deliberately linear:

    pa_20260715_analytics_facts
      -> sec_20260720_identity
      -> sec_20260720_boundaries
      -> sec_20260720_fresh
      -> sec_20260720_device

`sec_20260720_identity` starts the explicit 24-hour legacy email-token
compatibility window. `sec_20260720_boundaries` refuses to drop the plaintext
column until no migration-time compatibility token remains active. See
`docs/security-hardening-v2.md` for the staged commands and rollback limits.
`sec_20260720_fresh` adds nullable organization-bound KYC and inventory-listing
provenance without guessing legacy values. Its downgrade intentionally keeps
the compatible inventory fuel width at VARCHAR(20), so valid post-upgrade long
fuel values cannot break rollback.
`sec_20260720_device` adds nullable hashed browser-device ownership to refresh
families. Existing NULL-device rows are revoked, remain NULL, and are never
auto-bound; affected browsers must sign in again.

`env.py` fixes Alembic reflection to `public, pg_catalog`, excludes every
non-public extension schema, and dynamically discovers extension-owned or
extension-configured public relations from `pg_depend`, `pg_extension`,
`pg_class`, and `pg_namespace`. No Tiger/PostGIS relation list is hardcoded.
It also excludes explicitly enumerated database-only legacy
tables/order-delivery columns and preserves named historical performance
indexes/constraints that migrations own but ORM metadata does not; unknown
public application objects are not suppressed. It treats an existing
unbounded/wider VARCHAR as compatible with a bounded model declaration,
preventing unsafe shrink noise without hiding a database column that is
narrower than its model. Clean PostgreSQL 17/PostGIS 3.5 passes `alembic check`,
and a deliberate public application column drift remains detectable.

When integrating another migration branch, rebase/linearize these revisions
onto the chosen predecessor. Do not create an empty merge-head migration.
Always verify fresh upgrade, realistic upgrade through both security
revisions, supported downgrade/re-upgrade, one `alembic heads` result, and
`alembic check` before promotion.
